<h2>What the Registry scrutinises</h2>
<p>The Supreme Court Rules, 2013 commit the pre-registration examination of every filing to the Registry. Scrutiny is comprehensive: maintainability of the format rather than of the merits — whether the petition conforms to the prescribed form, whether the papers required by the Rules accompany it, whether it is presented by a person entitled to present it. The recurring heads of scrutiny include:</p>
<div class="check">
<ul>
  <li><strong>Presentation:</strong> filing through an Advocate-on-Record (or in person with the Registry's leave for petitioners-in-party), with the AoR's appearance and the vakalatnama in order;</li>
  <li><strong>Limitation:</strong> the computation disclosed, with an application for condonation of delay where the period has run;</li>
  <li><strong>The record:</strong> certified or web copies of the impugned judgment as the Rules and practice directions permit, annexures legible and in sequence, translations where documents are not in English;</li>
  <li><strong>Structure:</strong> the list of dates, the synopsis, the grounds without argumentative repetition, the prayer, and the affidavit properly sworn;</li>
  <li><strong>Court fee and formats:</strong> correct fee, prescribed paper and margins, pagination, indexing and e-filing compliance.</li>
</ul>
</div>

<h2>The life cycle of a filing</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Presentation.</strong> The petition is e-filed or tendered; a diary number issues. For limitation, this is the operative act of filing.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Scrutiny and defect notice.</strong> The Registry lists the defects — commonly running from a handful to dozens — with a period for curing. The list is available to the AoR through the Court's system.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Curing and refiling.</strong> The papers are corrected and refiled; fresh scrutiny follows; further notices issue if defects persist. Each cycle consumes days that aggregate into refiling delay.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. Registration and listing.</strong> On the Registry's satisfaction, the petition is registered, receives its case number, and proceeds to listing before the appropriate Bench.</div>
</div>

<h2>The defects that recur</h2>
<table class="law">
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Category</th><th>Typical instances</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Copies of the impugned order</td><td>Certified copy not filed and no exemption application; illegible copy; missing pages of the judgment</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Limitation disclosures</td><td>Incorrect computation in the list of dates; condonation application missing or not pressed into the correct period</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Affidavits and vakalatnama</td><td>Affidavit not sworn before the proper authority; deponent's authority not shown; vakalatnama unattested or unsigned at the required places</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Annexure discipline</td><td>Annexures not corresponding to the list of dates, unindexed, out of order, or in a vernacular without official translation</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Formal compliances</td><td>Deficit court fee; incorrect memo of parties; missing statement that no other similar petition is filed; e-filing metadata mismatches</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="note">
<p><strong>Practice note:</strong> The efficient chamber treats the defect notice as a checklist to be cleared in one pass, not seriatim. Cure every notified defect, re-verify the whole paper-book against the Rules while it is open — the second scrutiny examines afresh — and preserve a dated record of each refiling. If cumulative refiling time has grown for reasons outside counsel's control, prepare the explanatory application contemporaneously, while the reasons are documentable, rather than reconstructing them months later at the listing stage.</p>
</div>

<h2>Why refiling delay is taken seriously</h2>
<p>Limitation policy would be defeated if a party could stop the clock with a token filing and perfect the petition indefinitely. The Court's practice therefore watches the interval between the defect notice and the effective refiling. Modest intervals pass without comment; long ones — months of dormancy at the diary stage — call for explanation, and petitions have been declined on this ground alone. The discipline binds respondents too: counter-affidavits and applications pass through the same scrutiny, and a respondent's delays are equally visible on the record.</p>

<h2>Registrar's powers and the appeal within the Court</h2>
<p>The Rules vest the Registrar with the authority that makes scrutiny meaningful: to require parties to remove defects, to decline to receive petitions which are not in order, and to place recalcitrant filings before the Court. An aggrieved party is not remediless — the Rules provide an appeal from the Registrar's order declining to receive a petition, heard on the judicial side. In practice the remedy is rarely needed; dialogue with the Registry resolves most disputes about whether a requirement applies, and formal orders are reserved for genuine points of principle.</p>

<h2>The takeaway</h2>
<p>Nothing about Registry scrutiny is a formality: the diary-to-registration interval is part of the case's legal history, examined whenever limitation or diligence matters. The working rules are few and strict — file complete, cure in one pass, explain any unavoidable interval in writing at the time, and never use the diary number as a parking space. A petition that reaches its first listing with a clean procedural record argues from strength on the only question that ultimately counts: the merits.</p>
